@prefix tmk:     <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/> .
@prefix tmkc:    <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/concept/> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/evidence>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "TM-Knowledge — evidence module"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """**Still almost unpopulated — but for a different reason than the one this
comment used to give, and the difference is worth reading.**

Until 2026-09-12 this module said that populating it *"is a Part 22/23 job…
and Part 22 is explicitly out of the pilot scope draft"*. That sentence was
true when it was written and ADR-0081 falsified it on 2026-09-08: the section
43 boundary is withdrawn, the whole Manual is in scope, and there is no part of
this corpus a module may decline on scope grounds. A boundary that no longer
exists was still being cited here as a live reason four days later, which is
what a fence left in prose does (ADR-0110).

**The real reason, measured.** 226 chunks across 37 Parts mention evidence, so
the corpus has plenty to say. What the *vocabulary* holds is two concepts —
`GC-0041` and `GC-0101`, both labelled *evidence of use*, and they are one of
the ten duplicate-label pairs ADR-0101 put in front of the expert. Two concepts,
one of them a duplicate of the other, is not a taxonomy, and declaring classes
over it would produce a structure with nothing under it and an expert's name
nowhere near it.

So this module stays thin, and its emptiness is now a **gap with a size** —
the next concept pass over Parts 22, 23, 28, 34 and the opposition Parts is
what fills it — rather than a boundary. `tmk-ontology-report` counts every
class here that holds nothing."""@en-AU ;
    owl:imports <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/legal-concepts> ;
    owl:versionInfo "draft — not approved · unpopulated" .

tmk:Evidence a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNPOPULATED. Material put before the decision maker."@en-AU .

tmk:EvidenceCategory a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence category"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNPOPULATED. A kind of evidence, as the practice material groups them."@en-AU .

tmk:EvidenceSubmission a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence submission"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNPOPULATED. The act of filing evidence in a matter."@en-AU .

tmk:EvidentiaryProposition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalProposition ;
    rdfs:label "Evidentiary proposition"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """UNPOPULATED, and it must stay unpopulated while automated reasoning is bounded
as it is.

'The evidence establishes acquired distinctiveness' is an evaluative conclusion
and stays outside automated reasoning scope (queries/README.md). A class that
holds such propositions is fine; a rule that derives one is not."""@en-AU .

tmk:establishes a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Evidence ;
    rdfs:label "establishes"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNUSED. Declared to name the shape of the relation, not to license it: nothing in this repo may derive this edge."@en-AU .

# --- the three stages the Regulations name in terms -------------------------
#
# AUTHORED 2026-09-12 and read by nobody (ADR-0110). These three are not a
# proposed taxonomy of evidence: they are three phrases the corpus uses as
# terms of art — `evidence in support` in 16 chunks, `evidence in answer` in 7,
# `evidence in reply` in 6 — and the opposition Parts run them as an ordered
# sequence. Declaring them is closer to transcription than to modelling, which
# is the only reason a machine is declaring them at all.
#
# They hold nothing, and the report counts them among the classes that do. What
# they buy is a place for the opposition evidence stages to land that is not
# `tmk:Evidence` undifferentiated — and a visible, countable gap if nobody
# lands anything there.

tmk:EvidenceInSupport a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:EvidenceSubmission ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence in support"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNPOPULATED · AUTHORED, unreviewed. The opponent's evidence, filed first. Named in terms across the opposition Parts; no expert has read this declaration."@en-AU .

tmk:EvidenceInAnswer a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:EvidenceSubmission ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence in answer"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNPOPULATED · AUTHORED, unreviewed. The applicant's evidence, filed in answer to evidence in support. No expert has read this declaration."@en-AU .

tmk:EvidenceInReply a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:EvidenceSubmission ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence in reply"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNPOPULATED · AUTHORED, unreviewed. The opponent's evidence in reply, which closes the sequence. No expert has read this declaration."@en-AU .
